When the cube is pushed down by a small distance \(x\), the net restoring force is due to the extra buoyant force.
F_{\text{net}} = (L^2 x) \rho g
According to Newton's second law, \(F_{\text{net}} = ma_{\text{net}}\).
ma_{\text{net}} = L^2 x \rho g
So, the acceleration is:
a_{\text{net}} = \frac{L^2 \rho g}{m} x
Comparing this with the equation for simple harmonic motion \(a = \omega^2 x\), we get:
\omega^2 = \frac{L^2 \rho g}{m}
The time period of oscillation is \(T = \frac{2\pi}{\omega}\).
T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{m}{L^2 \rho g}}
Given values:
• Mass \(m = 10\text{g} = 0.01\text{ kg}\)
• Side length \(L = 10\text{ cm} = 0.1\text{ m}\)
• Density of water \(\rho = 1000\text{ kg/m}^3\)
• Acceleration due to gravity \(g = 10\text{ m/s}^2\)
Substitute these values into the time period formula:
T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{0.01}{(0.1)^2 \times 1000 \times 10}}
