\Delta W = m(\Delta a)_{\text{max}}
For SHM, the maximum acceleration is \(a_{\text{max}} = A\omega^2\). The total variation in acceleration from maximum upward to maximum downward is \(2A\omega^2\). Therefore, the maximum variation in weight is:
\Delta W = m \times 2\omega^2 A
Substitute \(\omega = \frac{2\pi}{T}\):
\Delta W = 2m \times \left(\frac{2\pi}{T}\right)^2 \times A
Given \(m = 50\) kg, \(T = 40\pi\) s, and \(A = 8\) m. Substitute these values:
\Delta W = 2 \times 50 \times \left(\frac{2\pi}{40\pi}\right)^2 \times 8 \text{ N}
\Delta W = 2 \times 50 \times \left(\frac{1}{20}\right)^2 \times 8 \text{ N}
\Delta W = 2 \times 50 \times \frac{1}{400} \times 8 \text{ N}
\Delta W = \frac{100 \times 8}{400} \text{ N}
\Delta W = \frac{800}{400} \text{ N}
\Delta W = 2 \text{ N}
